url: "https://evidencepress.org/releases/wide-binary-angle-inversion/"
abstract: "Unrefereed research candidate. For wide binary stars observed at a random orbital phase and random 3D orientation, the likelihood of the angle between projected separation and projected relative velocity is derived in closed form; its mixtures are Abel transforms, so the angle distribution determines the eccentricity distribution (including atoms), answering the uniqueness question in Appendix B of Hwang, Ting and Zakamska (2022). The inverse is weakly continuous but discontinuous in total variation. Moments such as E sqrt(1-e^2) = (pi/2) E sin^3 Gamma and E e^2 = 1/2 + E cos 2Gamma - (1/2) E cos 4Gamma need no inversion. Two exact selection results bear on Gaia samples. Synthetic checks only; no observational catalogue is analysed."
keywords:
  - wide binaries
  - orbital eccentricity
  - Gaia
  - projected angle
  - Abel transform
  - identifiability
  - inverse problems
  - Kepler orbits
